<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">J. G. Strijdom</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1893">1893</a>–<a href="/years/1958">1958</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Johannes Gerhardus Strijdom</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">J. G. Strijdom was a South African politician who served as prime minister from 1954 until his death in 1958. A hard-line <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afrikaners">Afrikaner</a> nationalist from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/transvaal">Transvaal</a>, he pushed apartheid further, removing coloured voters from the common roll in 1956 by enlarging the Senate and the Appellate Division, and promoted the goal of a republic. He was succeeded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hendrik-verwoerd">Hendrik Verwoerd</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/j-g-strijdom.jpg" alt="J. G. Strijdom" width="320" height="418" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hans_Strijdom_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Johannes Gerhardus Strijdom was born on 14 July 1893 on the family farm of Klipfontein near Willowmore in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cape-colony">Cape Colony</a>. His father, Petrus Strijdom, was a farmer and businessman in the Baviaanskloof. He trained as a lawyer at Victoria College and the University of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pretoria">Pretoria</a> and served in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/german-south-west-africa">German South West Africa</a> campaign in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a>, reaching the rank of corporal. He settled at Nylstroom in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/transvaal">Transvaal</a> and became a leader of the local <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afrikaners">Afrikaners</a>. In 1929 he was elected to the House of Assembly for Waterberg, representing the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/national-party-south-africa">National Party</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/j-b-m-hertzog">J. B. M. Hertzog</a>, and led the party in the Transvaal.</p><p>When Hertzog's party merged with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jan-smuts">Jan Smuts</a>'s South African Party in 1932 to form the United Party, Strijdom joined the breakaway Purified National Party under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/d-f-malan">D. F. Malan</a>, and he was the only member of parliament from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/transvaal">Transvaal</a> to support Malan. After the National Party's victory in 1948 he became Minister of Agriculture and Irrigation.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">In office</h2><p>Strijdom was elected leader of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/national-party-south-africa">National Party</a>, and so prime minister, on 30 November 1954, after Malan resigned and against Malan's wishes, for Malan had preferred the more moderate Nicolaas Havenga. Party members trusted him to move towards a republic. He began to sever ties with the British monarchy, deepened <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afrikaners">Afrikaner</a> ascendancy and strengthened apartheid, including through the Group Areas Development Act. He was an open proponent of baasskap, white domination, and believed in perpetuating white minority rule by removing Cape Coloured voters from the common roll.</p><p>The long Treason Trial of 156 activists, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nelson-mandela">Nelson Mandela</a> among them, took place during his term. His government broke off diplomatic relations with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/national-party-south-africa">National Party</a> increased its seats in the general election of 1958. His health was weak in his last year, with long absences.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Strijdom died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cape-town">Cape Town</a> on 24 August 1958 and was succeeded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hendrik-verwoerd">Hendrik Verwoerd</a>. He is buried in the Heroes' Acre in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pretoria">Pretoria</a>. The republic he favoured was established in 1961. Monuments to him once stood in several places: a bust in central Pretoria collapsed in 2001, injuring two people, and his house at Modimolle, formerly Nylstroom, is now a museum. The Hillbrow Tower in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johannesburg">Johannesburg</a> bore his name until 1995, and a street in Pretoria named after him was renamed in 2012.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “J. G. Strijdom” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Under <b>J. G. Strijdom</b> (1954–1958) and Hendrik Verwoerd (from 1958) the National Party deepened apartheid, removed Coloured voters from the common roll in 1956 after a long constitutional struggle, and planned separate ethnic ‘homelands’ under the Promotion of Bantu Self-Government Act of 1959.</blockquote><a href="/empire/union-of-south-africa">Union of South Africa · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Willowmore, in what is now <a href="/countries/south-africa">South Africa</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cape-town">Cape Town</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/south-africa">South Africa</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=-33.29,23.48&amp;year=1935" aria-label="Willowmore on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of J. G. Strijdom"><image href="/maps/globes/g030s30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4535 5294h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4535 5294h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=-33.29,23.48&amp;year=1935">What was here in 1935? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
